     description: Answer a question, in the person's own words.
     actor:

B.1.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
B.1.1. {"problem": "The diff turns ask_question from confirmed to proposed and drops its quote. The file shown after the change says status: confirmed, with a source ref (interview line L22) but no quote. Either the action is deployed as proposed, so it is not a settled rule, or it is confirmed with no recorded words of a person approving the expert's access. Every other confirmed element carries a quote or a record. The other interview refs in the file only reach L11, so L22 cannot be checked against them.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.ask_question (source and status)", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
B.1.2. {"problem": "The Question object description still says the question is 'for the owner to answer'. Experts can now ask questions, and answer_question already allows the expert, so the description no longer matches who asks and who answers.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, objects.Question description", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
B.1.3. {"problem": "The change widens the actors on a published action, but the domain version is still 10 and the composition still pins authoring interface 1. If this counts as a visible interface change, nothing records it. The change request also gives no reason for the change (the 'Why' line is empty).", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml version; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
B.2.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
B.2.1. {"problem": "The change moves ask_question's source to interview line L22 and drops the quote that justified it, and the diff also sets its status to proposed. In the file as it stands after the change, the action still says `status: confirmed`. A confirmed element must come from a person's confirmation, and nothing on record shows the owner agreeing to widen this action to the expert. The change request's 'Why:' is also empty. So the diff and the final file disagree, and the action is either confirmed without a quote or left unconfirmed in a declaration that is being deployed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.ask_question (source, status)", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
B.2.2. {"problem": "The Question object's description still says it is 'for the owner to answer'. The expert can now ask questions too, and answer_question already allows both owner and expert, so the description no longer matches how the domain behaves.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, objects.Question.description", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
B.2.3. {"problem": "The change widens who may call ask_question, which changes the authoring interface that Delivery and the composition depend on. The domain version is still 10 and the composition version is still 1, so the changed behaviour ships under the same version numbers. No scenario covers the expert asking a question.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml version; toprope-self.composition.yaml; scenarios", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
B.3.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
B.3.1. {"problem": "The diff sets ask_question to `status: proposed`, but the file after the change shows `status: confirmed`. Either the diff or the file is wrong. If the file is right, the rule is marked confirmed with a new source (interview L22) that has no quote, and nothing shows a person confirmed it. Every other confirmed interview-sourced element carries a quote. If the diff is right, the action is only proposed and the deployed domain behaves differently from what the file shows.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.ask_question (source/status)", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
B.3.2. {"problem": "The change removed the original source quote and ref (L5) and replaced them with a ref to interview L22 that has no quote. The reference cannot be checked here, and L22 is far beyond every other interview line the domain cites (L5–L11). The earlier evidence for who may ask questions is gone, and no evidence for the new expert permission is recorded. The change request's 'Why' is also empty.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.ask_question.source", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
B.3.3. {"problem": "The Question object description still says the question is 'for the owner to answer'. Experts can now ask questions, and answer_question already allows both owner and expert, so the wording no longer matches who asks and answers. The glossary and the other descriptions do not mention the expert asking.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, objects.Question.description", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
B.3.4. {"problem": "The new expert permission is not covered by any scenario. The existing scenarios only use the expert for mark_unknown, so the expert's new ability to ask a question is untested. The domain `version` is also left at 10 although the permitted actors changed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenarios / version", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
B.4.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
B.4.1. {"problem": "The diff sets ask_question to status: proposed, but the file after the change still says status: confirmed. The source was moved to interview line L22 and its quote was dropped. So either the action is confirmed with no quoted grounds for letting the expert ask, or the diff and the deployed file disagree about whether a person confirmed this. The change request gives no reason ('Why:' is empty), and the action's old confirmation was the L5 quote, which never covered the expert.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.ask_question (source and status)", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
B.4.2. {"problem": "The Question object is still described as 'A question in a stage's bundle, for the owner to answer.' Experts can now ask questions, and answer_question already allows owner and expert, so the description no longer matches who asks and who answers.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, objects.Question.description", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
B.4.3. {"problem": "A change to who may perform an action alters the authoring interface, but the domain version stays at 10, and the composition still pins authoring at interface 1. No scenario covers the expert asking a question.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, version; scenarios; toprope-self.composition.yaml, members.authoring.interface", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
B.5.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
B.5.1. {"problem": "The diff sets ask_question to `status: proposed`, but the file as it stands after the change says `status: confirmed`. The old quote is removed and the new source (interview L22) has no quote. The action therefore reads as confirmed with no human words behind it, and the diff and the file disagree about its status. Elsewhere only a person confirms an element (confirm_element requires human). Widening who may ask must stay proposed until the owner confirms it, with a quote.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.ask_question (source and status)", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
B.5.2. {"problem": "The old source and quote were replaced with a reference to interview line L22. Every other citation in the file points at L5–L11, and the diff does not show that L22 exists or says anything about experts asking questions. The change request gives no reason (its 'Why:' is empty). The new citation may not resolve and cannot justify the change.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.ask_question.source.ref", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
B.5.3. {"problem": "The Question object is still described as 'for the owner to answer', and the glossary and purpose text imply the owner is the one asking. Experts can now ask questions, but nothing updates that wording or says who answers an expert's question. answer_question already allows both owner and expert, so the roles on the two sides are now inconsistent with the description.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, objects.Question.description", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
B.5.4. {"problem": "The action's permissions were widened (a new actor on a declared action), which changes the domain's interface, but the domain `version` stays at 10. The composition pins authoring at interface 1, and the commits in the world carry interface versions such as 1.x. The change gives no sign that the new permission is versioned or that dependants are told.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, version / toprope-self.composition.yaml, members.authoring.interface", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
B.5.5. {"problem": "No scenario covers ask_question by the expert, or the full ask-then-answer path with the new role. The only expert scenario is a denied mark_unknown. The new permission is untested, so a mistake in it would not be caught before deployment.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenarios", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}

=== C ===
The edit: --- a/authoring/authoring.domain.yaml
+++ b/authoring/authoring.domain.yaml
@@ -39,7 +39,8 @@
       quote: 9번은 추천대로 하면 될 것 같고 10번도 추천대로 하면 될 것 같아. 일단 A로 가되 나중에 추가할 수 있는 거지?
     status: confirmed
   Question:
-    description: A question in a stage's bundle, for the owner to answer.
+    description: A question in a stage's bundle, asked by the owner or the expert,
+      for the owner to answer.
     properties:
       stage:
         type: string
@@ -287,6 +288,7 @@
     description: Ask a question of the current stage's bundle.
     actor:
     - owner
+    - expert
     creates: Question
     params:
       stage:

C.1.0. {"problem": "The Question description now says the question is asked by the owner or the expert \"for the owner to answer\". But answer_question already allows the expert to answer, and the Answer description still says \"The owner's answer\". The change did not reconcile these. They disagree about who answers, and the expert's questions now go into a bundle described as owner-only to answer.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, objects.Question description, objects.Answer description and actions.answer_question actor", "kind": "contradiction"}
C.1.1. {"problem": "The Question object has no property recording who asked it, so the new owner-or-expert distinction exists only in the description. Nothing in the data, and no scenario or fixture, shows or checks that an expert-asked question works. All world fixtures and scenarios still cover only the owner asking, and nothing exercises expert as an actor of ask_question.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, objects.Question properties; world.fixtures Q1; scenarios", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.1.2. {"problem": "The change adds a role to the actor list of a published action, which changes the declared interface. The domain version stays at 9, and the composition still pins interface 1, so the change reaches consumers with no version change to mark it.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, version; toprope-self.composition.yaml, members.authoring.interface", "kind": "other"}
C.2.0. {"problem": "The edited Question description says questions are 'for the owner to answer'. But answer_question lets both owner and expert answer, so an expert could answer a question they asked themselves. Answer still describes itself as 'The owner's answer, in their words', and nothing records who asked or restricts who answers. The change opens asking to the expert without reconciling these two descriptions or the answering rule.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: Question.description vs actions.answer_question.actor and Answer.description", "kind": "contradiction"}
C.2.1. {"problem": "ask_question now has a new actor (expert), which widens the authoring interface, but the domain version stays at 9. Delivery and the composition pin authoring to interface 1. Without a bump, the new permission is not distinguishable from the previously checked version.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: version, actions.ask_question.actor", "kind": "other"}
C.2.2. {"problem": "The ask_question source still cites the old generic interview line (L5). It says nothing about the expert asking questions, so the confirmed rule has no grounding for the new permission. No scenario covers an expert asking a question.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.ask_question.source and scenarios", "kind": "other"}
C.3.0. {"problem": "The change lets the expert ask a question, but answer_question already allows both owner and expert. The expert can now ask a question and answer it themselves, with no owner involved. The Question description says the question is 'for the owner to answer', and Answer is described as 'The owner's answer'. Both now contradict the actions. Answer decisions become proposed elements, so an expert-only loop can feed the draft. Either restrict who may answer the expert's questions or update the descriptions to match.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: actions.answer_question (actor owner+expert) vs objects.Question and objects.Answer descriptions", "kind": "contradiction"}
C.3.1. {"problem": "The Question description now says it is asked by the owner or the expert, but Question has no property that records who asked it. Nothing can tell an owner's question from an expert's, so the new distinction cannot be seen, filtered or routed.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: objects.Question properties", "kind": "other"}
C.3.2. {"problem": "No scenario covers the new permission, for example the expert asking a question or the owner then answering it. The existing scenarios do not exercise ask_question for the expert role.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: scenarios", "kind": "other"}
C.3.3. {"problem": "The authoring domain's declared interface changes, because ask_question gains a new permitted actor, but 'version: 9' is unchanged. The composition pins 'interface: 1' without a matching version bump or review of the member.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: version; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring", "kind": "other"}
C.3.4. {"problem": "ask_question is still marked confirmed and cites the old interview quote ('9번은 추천대로...'). That quote does not support the expert asking questions, so the new rule has no confirming source in the interview and is marked confirmed without one.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: actions.ask_question source/status", "kind": "other"}
C.4.0. {"problem": "The change rewrites the Question description to say a question is asked by the owner or the expert \"for the owner to answer\". The answer_question action, which this change leaves alone, already lets both owner and expert answer. The description is therefore wrong about who answers, and it now reads as if an expert's question is routed to the owner. That is the routing the change request wants to remove.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, objects.Question.description vs actions.answer_question.actor", "kind": "contradiction"}
C.4.1. {"problem": "Adding expert to ask_question changes who may call an existing action, but the domain `version: 9` is not bumped. The ask_question source still cites the old generic interview quote, and no scenario covers an expert asking a question. The new permission has no recorded evidence and is not exercised by any scenario. The composition pins authoring at interface 1, and a version bump may be needed there.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.ask_question (source, scenarios) and version", "kind": "other"}
C.5.0. {"problem": "The new Question description says questions are asked 'for the owner to answer'. But answer_question is still open to both owner and expert, and Answer is described as 'The owner's answer, in their words'. The change makes the expert a full participant in asking, yet the declared text still says only the owner answers. Either the answerer wording or the answer_question actors is now wrong, and an expert-asked question can be answered by the expert who asked it.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: objects.Question.description vs actions.answer_question.actor and objects.Answer.description", "kind": "contradiction"}
C.5.1. {"problem": "ask_question now allows the expert, but a Question records no asker (only stage and text). Nothing in the domain shows whether the owner or the expert asked a question, so the new distinction exists only in the description. No scenario covers an expert asking.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: objects.Question.properties, actions.ask_question, scenarios", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.5.2. {"problem": "ask_question is still marked status: confirmed and cites the old generic interview quote, which says nothing about letting the expert ask. The new expert permission is therefore recorded as confirmed without evidence or a person's confirmation.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.ask_question.source/status", "kind": "other"}
C.5.3. {"problem": "The set of actors allowed on a published action changed, but the domain version stays 9 and the composition still pins interface 1. A permission change is not signalled to anything that depends on the old interface.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: version; toprope-self.composition.yaml: members.authoring.interface", "kind": "other"}
